{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"arbitrary-nautical-spreadwing","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## The problem\n\n`.github/workflows/test.yml:340-344` currently reads:\n\n```yaml\n  # Windows compatibility (epic 5664 S5). rebar is git- and path-heavy and the gate\n  # runs only Linux + macOS; this sweeps Windows so a path-separator / temp-dir /\n  # file-lock regression surfaces off the hot path. Non-blocking: Windows is not yet a\n  # declared support target, so a genuine incompatibility is a visible finding, never a\n  # red gate.\n```\n\nUnder the operator's adoption of option (a) on `f850-674c-c89f-4b97`, **\"Windows is not yet a\ndeclared support target\" is false.** The tree currently asserts the opposite of the decision, and\nwill keep asserting it for the whole life of this epic unless corrected now.\n\n## Why this is a separate, UNBLOCKED, first-landing task\n\nThis is the one child that is not blocked by S1. It changes no code and no job behaviour — the job\nstays `continue-on-error: true` — so it can land immediately and costs nothing. Its whole value is\nthat it changes what a reader concludes from the red badge: from **\"ignorable, nobody intends to fix\nthis\"** to **\"a known-incomplete declared support target being driven to green under epic\n`simple-brotherly-peacock`\"**.\n\nThat distinction is the entire signal-quality complaint of `f850`. Leaving it unrecorded for months\nwould reproduce the original defect under a new justification.\n\n## Scope — what to change, and what NOT to change\n\n**Do change:** the comment block. It must say Windows IS a declared support target, that the job is\ntemporarily non-voting because the epic is in flight, name the epic and the ADR, and state that the\n`continue-on-error` removal is the epic's exit criterion.\n\n**Do NOT change:** `continue-on-error: true`, the `if:` schedule/dispatch condition, or anything in\n`.github/workflows/gerrit-verify.yaml`. Flipping the job to gating now would redden every change\nwith 510 unrelated failures and destroy the `Verified` signal — that is S11's job, at the end, under\nADR 0114.\n\n## Proving command\n\n```\ngrep -n \"not yet a declared support target\" .github/workflows/test.yml   # must return nothing\ngrep -n \"continue-on-error: true\" .github/workflows/test.yml             # must still match sweep-windows\npython -c \"import yaml,sys; yaml.safe_load(open('.github/workflows/test.yml'))\"\n```\nplus the repo's existing CI-workflow-parity pytest selection:\n```\nuv run --no-sync pytest -k \"workflow\" -m \"not integration and not external\" -q -p no:cacheprovider\n```\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. `test.yml` contains no statement that Windows is not a support target.\n2. The comment names the epic and the ADR, and states that removing `continue-on-error` is the exit\n   criterion.\n3. The job's behaviour is unchanged: still schedule/dispatch-gated, still `continue-on-error: true`.\n","id":"4530-95dc-11b2-45ac","parent_id":"55da-cee1-e556-4735","priority":2,"tags":["windows","portability","ci","signal-quality"],"ticket_type":"task","title":"S10: test.yml:340-344 asserts Windows is not a support target — that comment is now FALSE and must be corrected"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1788571466378142217,"uuid":"eb5a9fe7-0268-4388-b633-9c9c4ba9317e"}